      "title": "Identifying Matrices in Row Echelon Form"
    },
    "question": {
      ":question/id": "9305bff4-0d1c-58bf-bc77-d264f660be96",
      ":question/problem": "Which of the following matrices are **not** in row echelon form?\n\nSelect all that apply.",
      ":question/worked-solution": "Remember that a matrix is in row echelon form if it satisfies the following conditions:\n\n- All the zero rows (rows with only zeros), if any, are placed at the *bottom* of the matrix.\n- Each leading entry of a row is in a column *to the right* of the leading entry of the row above it.\n\nWith that in mind, let's look at each matrix in turn.\n\n- The matrix $A$ is in row echelon form (both conditions are satisfied).\n\n$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}-9 & -2 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 5 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 8 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\n- The matrix $B$ is in row echelon form (both conditions are satisfied).\n\n$$\nB=\\begin{bmatrix}9 & -8 & -5 \\\\ 0 & -3 & -5 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 9 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\n- The matrix $C$ is in row echelon form (both conditions are satisfied).\n\n$$\nC=\\begin{bmatrix}-7 & 0 & 4 \\\\ 0 & -7 & 7 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nTherefore, all three matrices are in row echelon form.",
      ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/easy",
      ":question/answer-fields": [
        {
          ":answer-field/id": "fd9e8169-6dea-53e9-932c-275d95c19350",
